From 1da72372096bb1f3d460d59a5d5df1359917d434 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 23:48:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ace: generate module Makefiles from JSON manifests

Four hand-written module Makefiles gave four different answers to the same
questions, and every disagreement was silent:

  - DatabaseProvider's DEPFILE rule had no order-only dependency on its
    sqlite bootstrap, so -include ran the -MM preprocess before sqlite3.h
    existed: a fatal error followed by a successful retry.
  - Only NetworkProvider stamped vendored artifacts with the build arch.
    The others relink objects built for the machine the tree came from.
  - DatabaseProvider is a shared-layout module (it has OS/) carrying the
    per-platform template, so its header glob resolves to Linux/*.h, matches
    nothing, and its implementation headers have never been covered by the
    manifest hashes.
  - Three modules put vendored archives ahead of the translation unit that
    needs them; gold does not rescan, so that works by accident.

A manifest declares what a module needs, what it makes, and where that goes;
the generator emits all four fixes by construction, for every module.

Also the submission path: modules are accepted as manifests, not Makefiles.
build.system is a closed enum with no shell escape, and an unpinned ref is
refused outright -- an arbitrary command is the raw Makefile this format
exists to replace, and a floating ref cannot be reviewed.

  ace manifest { list | check | show <mod> | generate [mod...] [--force] }

Manifests live with the tool, not the tree, so a module that is a symlink
into a home directory resolves the same way as one in modules/. Generated
Makefiles are gitignored and regenerated on the build path when missing.
Manifest-declared system packages fold into `ace deps check`, reusing the
existing binary:/pkgconfig:/header: probe vocabulary unchanged.

Four modules ported. Every generated Makefile parses; the emitter is covered
for platform ordering (Web must precede Linux -- emscripten still reports
uname Linux) and for gating vendored objects behind their own fetch/build.
